<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Yorkshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> is a metropolitan county of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, created in 1974 and including Leeds, Bradford, Wakefield, Huddersfield and Halifax. It grew in the 18th and 19th centuries as the centre of the English wool textile industry. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> much of it lay within the British kingdom of Elmet.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-yorkshire.jpg" alt="West Yorkshire" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tim Green · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aspley_Basin_(edited,_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> covers 2,029 km2 and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-yorkshire">North Yorkshire</a>, South Yorkshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbyshire">Derbyshire</a>, Greater Manchester and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>. Its western part lies in the South Pennines, with steep valleys and a small part of the Peak District National Park, and the land flattens eastward towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-york">Vale of York</a>. The rivers Aire and Calder drain the county from west to east. Geologically it rests almost entirely on rocks of carboniferous age, including the Yorkshire coalfield, with younger Magnesian Limestone in the extreme east.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county was formed in 1974 under the Local Government Act 1972 from the central part of the West Riding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, and it corresponds roughly to the historic core of the Riding and the county boroughs of Bradford, Dewsbury, Halifax, Huddersfield, Leeds and Wakefield. Its five metropolitan boroughs are Bradford, Calderdale, Kirklees, Leeds and Wakefield. The Local Government Act 1985 abolished the county council in 1986 and passed its powers to the boroughs and to joint bodies, but West Yorkshire remains a metropolitan and ceremonial county with a Lord Lieutenant and a High Sheriff.</p><p>Wakefield's parish church became a cathedral in 1888 and the town was granted city status that July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> then made Leeds and Bradford the area's two largest cities; Leeds received city status in 1893 and Bradford in 1897. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> was mined around Wakefield, Castleford, Pontefract and South and East Leeds. Bradford, Halifax and Huddersfield grew through woollen mills, Leeds was a centre of cloth manufacture, and the Heavy Woollen District included Dewsbury, Batley, Morley, Ossett, Cleckheaton and Heckmondwike. The woollen and cloth industries declined through the twentieth century, and the last colliery in the county, Hay Royds at Denby Dale, closed in 2012 after a flood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Leeds is the largest settlement, and the five boroughs work together through the West Yorkshire Combined Authority, established in 2014. The Rhubarb Triangle, wholly in the county, still produces forced rhubarb, which received protected designation of origin status in 2010. Saltaire is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and other landmarks include Kirkstall Abbey, Pontefract Castle, Sandal Castle, Harewood House and the Brontë Parsonage Museum at Haworth. The Middleton Railway is described as the world's oldest steam railway.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Yorkshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…has appeared on screen from Secret of the Incas (1954), the first major Hollywood film shot on location there, and Werner Herzog's Aguirre, the Wrath of God (1972) to The Motorcycle Diaries (2004), and it is twinned with Haworth in <b>West Yorkshire</b>, Petra in Jordan and other places.</blockquote><a href="/work/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Elmet emerged after Roman rule ended as a British kingdom in the area of modern <b>West Yorkshire</b>, between the Pennines and the Vale of York.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-elmet">Kingdom of Elmet</a></li></ul></section>
